sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In physical chaotic systems such as kneading, mirror arrangements or pegboards, randomness can be attributed purely to initial conditions only for a small number of steps (around ten collisions for a pegboard), because amplification quickly reaches atomic scales and environmental perturbations take over; the limit is set by the separation between observed large-scale and uncontrollable small-scale features.";
    dc:type "model-of-nature";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Randomness from initial conditions lasts only a limited number of steps";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “Chaos Theory and Randomness from Initial Conditions” (from p. 304), p. 310. Scope: physical systems with sensitive dependence on initial conditions"
}
